A||||7||Ozzy||'88 coupe steering rack problem...
Z||000000||Ozzy||09-02-99||08:16 PM||||Hey folks... Well, life with a Fiero is anything but boring.<p>I recently posted my success with installing new LBJ's, urethane bushings and shocks. I had some slop in the front end causing a somewhat eratic and slightly unpredictable stering problem. I have had a wheel alignment after doing this. I also thought I had identified the lose or worn parts in the ball joints but...<p>It still seems to dart off slightly at highway speed so I jacked the front tonight and started looking for loose wheel bearings or something. The left wheel bearing has just over 1/8" slop. Thats easy and cheap. The other thing I noticed is the loose tie rod. If I grab the left & right side of the Left wheel moving it laterly back and forth I can hear and see something like a loose joint somewhere inside the boot on the tie rod. Probably another 1/8" or more slop. I'm fairly sure this is the problem but what can I do about the rack. Are there replaceable parts under that boot or do I have to replace the whole darn rack?? And why didn't they pick that up when doing the alignment?<p>If I have to change the rack, how much can I get a good replacemnet for. This is a high ratio I think. 3 1/4 turns lock to lock.<p>Your thoughts and input are appreciated.<p>Ozzy<br>
Z||000001||batboy||09-02-99||09:09 PM||||The inner tie rod ends can be replaced, but it requires a special tool. A good shop that specializes in front end work should be able to replace them for you.
Z||000002||Ozzy||09-03-99||10:06 AM||||So far I'm hearing I need to replace the rack 'cause the plastic bushings are probably worn too. The Fiero Store quoted me catalogue pricing on a Reman Rack & pinion without inners. $229.95. Inners @ $63.75.<p>Where else can I look? Is it worth just swapping out the inner tie rod ends? <p>Also I'm being told that the Front wheel bearing assy. is part of the whole hub which will cost $110.65 from the Fiero Store.<br>Before long I'm gonna have $1,000.00 into this front end at these prices. Thats o.k. if thats what it takes...I just don't wanna get 'Took'!<p>Ozzy
Z||000003||batboy||09-03-99||03:49 PM||||I don't know much about the Fiero steering racks, but I do know those inner tie rods do wear out ($50 each for Moog brand tie rods ends here locally.<p>Maybe the 1988 Fieros had a sealed wheel bearing assembly, but all the other years didn't. I replaced all the front bearings and seals on my 85 SE for about $40.<br>
Z||000004||Ozzy||09-04-99||12:25 PM||||O.K. Now I'm confused! The Fiero shop from Haward, CA. Is quoting me $100.00 for a replacement steering rack, and also telling me you CAN replace the bearings only on the front hubs of an '88 coupe. Which is it?<p>ANYBODY???<p>Ozzy
Z||000005||K9||09-04-99||04:42 PM||||Bearings- I think you should be able to replace the bearings only with out the hub. Contact your local parts shop and find out if they sell it without the hub. I did my 85 GT at a cost of $10 Canadian per side. Good luck.  
Z||000006||Ozzy||09-05-99||09:35 PM||||Just thought I'd give the latest on this although even though this topic did not seem to create much interest.<p>After talking to George (Owner) of the Fiero shop of the SF Bay,CA.<p>http://fieroshop.net/ (Cool dude and very helpfull.)<p>I found out that the plastic bushings are a common wear item in the ends of the steering rack. I proceeded to remove the boot from the right side tie rod and found the inner tie rod end to be fine but the suspect 'plastic' bushing to be worn significantly enough to create the slop in the steering mechanism. I'm told that replacement bushings can be bought from the dealer and I have seen in a post somewhere of having brass bushings machined which is probably what I'm going to do. To temporarily solve the problem I was able to create shims from an aluminium can. I left the rack on the vehichle and managed to move the bushing out onto the rack. I then neatly cut a 1 1/4" inch wide strip from a can (watch yer fingers!)and greased it then wrapped one layer around the rack and carefully slid the plastic bushing over it. I then tabbed the excess on each side to stop it from sliding out. I also used a double thickness to shim the outer of the bushing where it locates in the rack housing. This is only a very temporary fix intended to make driving more comfortable untill I find some brass replacements. Having said that, this made a big difference. I now hove some idea of how a Fiero should drive!<p>Ozzy<p>
Z||000007||mwbackus||09-05-99||10:58 PM||||All 88 Fieros have sealed bearing assemblies both front and back.  New fronts cost over $100 each.  Sorry to be the bearer of bad news.  :-(<p>Mike
